WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.910
Well, it kind of beings in the funniest way on the City of Cambridge side we

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:19.700
have wonderful former City Councilor and
Mayor Al Vellucci, who was my mentor who ...

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:27.590
he was a big idea guy. Although he
didn't graduate from high school until

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:30.619
he was in his sixties. He went to

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:38.600
Community Learning Center and got a GED. But, Al liked to be kind of a prankster and a

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:43.010
trickster with Harvard and with MIT. So
he would threaten at various times to

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:49.340
pave over Harvard Square or Harvard Yard. You know ... they should pay taxes and he

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:53.170
would kind of have his own critique. And in that way he

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:59.930
exercised a fair amount of influence with
Harvard particularly. With MIT he

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:05.320
kind of got into this interesting
question about "what exactly are they doing

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:20.130
in all these labs?" Particularly, labs with animals. Are their petri dishes producing 3-headed people? And that kind of thing.

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:27.719
Of course the scientific community got a little concerned that nobody has

00:01:27.719 --> 00:01:34.289
really asked this. But Al, in an attempt to
answer his own question,

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:41.020
he got involved with understanding about the National Institutes of Health and how much money

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:47.840
they give for research how much
disproportionately MIT gets. And so,

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:57.490
I can't tell you exactly how he made this leap, but he got the Cambridge City Council to adopt the research

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:03.609
standards produced by the National
Institutes of Health, and really this gave

00:02:03.609 --> 00:02:09.660
scientists both here and from elsewhere
the first place that they knew what the

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:15.090
rules were. And this began to attract
various researchers

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:23.849
here in and in companies, and then MIT
literally a very intimate

00:02:23.849 --> 00:02:27.530
relationship with the National
Institutes of Health. And they, as I said

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:35.910
before, disproportionally received
funding that way. And in fact, MIT

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:40.010
determines how many graduate students it's going to have in the sciences largely based on

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:45.569
what the research funding is from NIH, 
and then that research often walks across

00:02:45.569 --> 00:02:54.590
the street to a for-profit company
founded by the MIT professor who worked

00:02:54.590 --> 00:03:02.420
on the NIH grant. And I and it turns out that this is very much in the DNA of MIT.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:08.040
At Harvard it would just be unthinkable, prior to now--Harvard has changed its

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:13.959
thinking--that you would mix "for-profit
science" with "pure science." Well, at MIT

00:03:13.959 --> 00:03:24.170
that's been good always. And hence, it really is in the DNA here. And so that has caused so many

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:28.400
different companies to be spun off from
work done at MIT.

